1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,180
A parcel in bubble wrap.

2
00:00:02,180 --> 00:00:04,266
A coconut with husk and shell.

3
00:00:04,266 --> 00:00:06,204
A bacterium on a lab slide.

4
00:00:06,604 --> 00:00:11,319
Each one is protected by layers, outside
layers wrapped around the inside.

5
00:00:11,769 --> 00:00:14,708
In the lab, Diya stained
bacteria on a slide.

6
00:00:14,708 --> 00:00:17,105
Some turned purple, some turned pink.

7
00:00:17,105 --> 00:00:19,361
She asked: what wraps a bacterium?

8
00:00:19,361 --> 00:00:21,710
And why do bacteria stain differently?

9
00:00:22,060 --> 00:00:22,960
Two answers.

10
00:00:22,952 --> 00:00:26,305
A bacterium is wrapped in a
three layered cell envelope.

11
00:00:26,305 --> 00:00:30,357
And the envelope differs, so bacteria
take up the stain differently.

12
00:00:30,857 --> 00:00:34,983
By the end, you will know the cell
envelope, the mesosome, flagella, pili

13
00:00:34,983 --> 00:00:38,407
and fimbriae, and the ribosomes
and stores inside a bacterium.

14
00:00:39,057 --> 00:00:41,000
First, meet the cell envelope.

15
00:00:41,350 --> 00:00:45,868
Most prokaryotic cells, especially
bacteria, have a cell envelope with

16
00:00:45,868 --> 00:00:48,464
three layers, tightly bound together.

17
00:00:48,914 --> 00:00:53,387
From outside to inside: the glycocalyx,
then the cell wall, then the plasma

18
00:00:53,387 --> 00:00:54,287
membrane.

19
00:00:54,083 --> 00:00:58,519
Each layer has its own job, but together
they act as one protective unit.

20
00:00:59,169 --> 00:01:01,251
Let's zoom into one bacterium.

21
00:01:01,601 --> 00:01:02,946
The whole bacterium.

22
00:01:02,946 --> 00:01:07,405
On its surface you can see a long
flagellum, and many small hairs, called

23
00:01:07,405 --> 00:01:08,656
pili and fimbriae.

24
00:01:09,156 --> 00:01:10,434
Zoom into the edge.

25
00:01:10,434 --> 00:01:14,540
Here are the three layers of the
envelope, the glycocalyx, the cell wall

26
00:01:14,540 --> 00:01:16,039
and the plasma membrane.

27
00:01:16,539 --> 00:01:18,376
Zoom again, into the cytoplasm.

28
00:01:18,376 --> 00:01:21,415
Here are the ribosomes, tiny
bodies that make proteins.

29
00:01:21,415 --> 00:01:23,987
Each one is about fifteen
by twenty nanometres.

30
00:01:23,987 --> 00:01:27,118
A nanometre is a thousand times
smaller than a micrometre.

31
00:01:27,618 --> 00:01:32,243
Each ribosome has two parts, a large
fifty S part and a small thirty S

32
00:01:32,243 --> 00:01:33,143
part.

33
00:01:32,812 --> 00:01:35,679
Together, they form a seventy S ribosome.

34
00:01:36,329 --> 00:01:38,597
Now the three layers, step by step.

35
00:01:38,947 --> 00:01:39,847
Step one.

36
00:01:39,629 --> 00:01:41,717
The glycocalyx is the outermost layer.

37
00:01:41,717 --> 00:01:45,185
In some bacteria it is a loose
sheath, called the slime layer.

38
00:01:45,935 --> 00:01:46,835
Step two.

39
00:01:46,619 --> 00:01:49,574
In other bacteria, the
glycocalyx is thick and tough.

40
00:01:49,574 --> 00:01:51,335
Then it is called the capsule.

41
00:01:52,085 --> 00:01:52,985
Step three.

42
00:01:52,906 --> 00:01:55,297
The cell wall gives the cell its shape.

43
00:01:55,297 --> 00:01:59,330
It gives strong support, so the
bacterium does not burst or collapse.

44
00:02:00,080 --> 00:02:00,980
Step four.

45
00:02:00,886 --> 00:02:03,210
The plasma membrane is semi permeable.

46
00:02:03,210 --> 00:02:07,841
It lets some things through and not
others, and it is built much like ours.

47
00:02:08,591 --> 00:02:09,491
Step five.

48
00:02:09,419 --> 00:02:11,713
The envelope decides the Gram stain.

49
00:02:11,713 --> 00:02:15,140
Bacteria that take up the
Gram stain are Gram positive.

50
00:02:15,140 --> 00:02:17,491
Those that do not are Gram negative.

51
00:02:18,241 --> 00:02:19,141
Remember this.

52
00:02:19,103 --> 00:02:22,188
Glycocalyx, cell wall, plasma
membrane, from outside in.

53
00:02:22,788 --> 00:02:25,682
Now a special part of the
membrane, the mesosome.

54
00:02:26,032 --> 00:02:29,541
The mesosome is formed when the
plasma membrane folds into the cell.

55
00:02:29,541 --> 00:02:33,370
These folds can be vesicles, tubules
or lamellae, which are flat sheets.

56
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:37,053
The mesosome helps in making the
cell wall, in copying the DNA, and in

57
00:02:37,053 --> 00:02:39,222
sharing the DNA between
the two daughter cells.

58
00:02:39,672 --> 00:02:43,853
It also helps in respiration and
secretion, and it gives the membrane more

59
00:02:43,853 --> 00:02:45,704
surface area and more enzymes.

60
00:02:45,704 --> 00:02:49,434
In cyanobacteria, other folds
called chromatophores hold pigments.

61
00:02:50,084 --> 00:02:52,628
Now the parts that stick
out from the surface.

62
00:02:52,978 --> 00:02:54,574
Some bacteria can swim.

63
00:02:54,574 --> 00:02:57,513
They move with thin
threads, called flagella.

64
00:02:57,513 --> 00:03:01,873
A flagellum has three parts: a
filament, a hook and a basal body.

65
00:03:01,873 --> 00:03:04,072
The filament is the longest part.

66
00:03:04,522 --> 00:03:09,179
Pili and fimbriae are surface hairs
too, but they do not help in moving.

67
00:03:09,179 --> 00:03:12,183
Pili are long tubes made
of a special protein.

68
00:03:12,633 --> 00:03:15,243
Fimbriae are small, bristle like fibres.

69
00:03:15,243 --> 00:03:19,663
In some bacteria, they help the cell
stick to rocks in streams, and to

70
00:03:19,663 --> 00:03:21,252
the tissues of a host.

71
00:03:21,902 --> 00:03:23,493
Back inside the bacterium.

72
00:03:23,493 --> 00:03:26,363
Its ribosomes are attached
to the plasma membrane.

73
00:03:26,713 --> 00:03:30,494
Several ribosomes can sit on one
messenger RNA, like beads on a thread.

74
00:03:30,494 --> 00:03:34,606
This chain is a polysome, and its ribosomes
read the message to make proteins.

75
00:03:35,056 --> 00:03:38,967
The cell also stores food and minerals
in inclusion bodies, with no membrane

76
00:03:38,967 --> 00:03:39,867
around them.

77
00:03:39,769 --> 00:03:43,688
Examples are phosphate granules,
cyanophycean granules and glycogen granules.

78
00:03:43,688 --> 00:03:46,391
Some photosynthetic bacteria
also have gas vacuoles.

79
00:03:46,991 --> 00:03:47,891
Your turn.

80
00:03:47,873 --> 00:03:51,829
Which helps a bacterium swim:
its pili, or its flagellum?

81
00:03:51,829 --> 00:03:53,127
Pause and decide.

82
00:03:56,327 --> 00:03:57,259
The flagellum!

83
00:03:57,259 --> 00:03:59,638
Pili and fimbriae do not help in moving.

84
00:04:00,288 --> 00:04:01,847
Now, a worked example.

85
00:04:01,847 --> 00:04:03,655
Name the part from its job.

86
00:04:04,005 --> 00:04:04,905
Job one.

87
00:04:04,715 --> 00:04:06,985
It stops the bacterium from bursting.

88
00:04:06,985 --> 00:04:08,507
That is the cell wall.

89
00:04:08,957 --> 00:04:09,857
Job two.

90
00:04:09,634 --> 00:04:12,438
It helps the bacterium
stick to a host's tissues.

91
00:04:12,438 --> 00:04:13,791
That is the fimbriae.

92
00:04:14,242 --> 00:04:15,142
Job three.

93
00:04:15,049 --> 00:04:19,346
It increases the surface area of the
membrane, and helps copy the DNA.

94
00:04:19,796 --> 00:04:20,802
That is the mesosome.

95
00:04:21,452 --> 00:04:22,916
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:23,265 --> 00:04:27,728
Many students add fifty S and thirty
S, and say the bacterial ribosome

97
00:04:27,728 --> 00:04:28,766
is eighty S.

98
00:04:29,166 --> 00:04:31,405
But S units do not simply add up.

99
00:04:31,405 --> 00:04:35,620
Together, the fifty S and thirty
S parts make a seventy S ribosome.

100
00:04:36,270 --> 00:04:37,488
Where is this asked?

101
00:04:37,488 --> 00:04:41,135
As statement questions on the envelope
layers, mesosome jobs and ribosome

102
00:04:41,135 --> 00:04:42,035
units.

103
00:04:42,233 --> 00:04:43,247
Exam level now.

104
00:04:43,247 --> 00:04:45,531
Which statement about mesosomes is wrong?

105
00:04:45,531 --> 00:04:46,587
Pause and solve.

106
00:04:51,987 --> 00:04:53,373
The answer is D.

107
00:04:53,873 --> 00:04:56,031
Options A, B and C are all true.

108
00:04:56,031 --> 00:05:00,284
Mesosomes help make the cell wall,
copy and share the DNA, and add surface

109
00:05:00,284 --> 00:05:01,651
area to the membrane.

110
00:05:02,151 --> 00:05:06,314
But mesosomes are folds of the plasma
membrane, not of the cell wall.

111
00:05:06,314 --> 00:05:08,141
So D is the wrong statement.

112
00:05:08,741 --> 00:05:10,281
Here is your recap.

113
00:05:10,281 --> 00:05:11,864
Screenshot this card.

114
00:05:15,064 --> 00:05:17,250
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

115
00:05:17,650 --> 00:05:18,550
One.

116
00:05:18,141 --> 00:05:22,008
Name the three layers of the
cell envelope, from outside in.

117
00:05:22,908 --> 00:05:23,808
Two.

118
00:05:23,307 --> 00:05:25,441
What are the three parts of a flagellum?

119
00:05:26,341 --> 00:05:27,241
Three.

120
00:05:26,835 --> 00:05:29,130
Which two units make a seventy S ribosome?

121
00:05:30,230 --> 00:05:34,558
Next time: why does oil never mix with
water, and how does that build the

122
00:05:34,558 --> 00:05:35,856
skin of every cell?

123
00:05:35,856 --> 00:05:37,948
We find out in the cell membrane.
